            <ArticleTitle>Clinical evaluation of a bioactive beta-glucan gel in the treatment of 'hard-to-heal' wounds.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>58-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.12968/jowc.2017.26.2.58</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this evaluation is to assess the effects of a wound healing gel in a wounds of different aetiologies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data was recorded on the wound surface area, tissue type, and patient level of wound pain at baseline (0) and at weeks 1, 2, 3, 4, and 8.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of the total 39 patients enrolled in the study, 26 patients who complied with the protocol criteria completed the minimum four-week study period. During the 12-week evaluation period, seven of the 26 wounds fully healed and an additional eight wounds showed a reduction in size of more than 50 %. Of the remaining 11, five wounds showed moderate healing progression and six wounds did not respond to treatment. Following the 12 week evaluation time point clinicians reported that a further three wounds healed-a 38 % healing rate.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results give promise that this advanced gel, containing a macrophage activating substance, can be a tool in re-activating healing in stalled wounds where standard of care is no longer giving the desired healing progression.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
